      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:16u5gej'
    '|a||And at two per cent? Twice the rate, so fourteen times, I suppose.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5040
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:t2ss3k'
    '|b||Keep a ghost of the one per cent path, and watch. After the same two hundred years: fifty-two times richer.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6960
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:10etz7'
    '|a||So one extra percentage point turns seven into fifty-two.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4560
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:epwgua'
    '|b||A handy rule: divide seventy by the growth rate to get the years it takes to double. At two per cent, that is thirty-five years: about twice in a lifetime.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9920
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1bbylla'
    '|a||That is why economists obsess over a single percentage point: over a few generations it is the difference between staying poor and getting rich.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9440
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||If you want to see where the seventy comes from, open the math; if not, skip it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4680
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1muzi40'
    '|b||Where the rule of 70 comes from: open the math, or skip it?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3080
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:13o5ylp'
    '|a||If you chose the math: doubling means one plus g, multiplied T times, equals two.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6600
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|a||Take logs: T times the log of one plus g equals the log of two, about 0.69.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6440
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:saxfu6'
    '|a||For small g, the log of one plus g is close to g itself.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4440
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1ny4wpi'
    '|a||So T is about 0.69 over g: seventy over the rate in per cent.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6280
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:x2wtdt'
    '|a||So the whole question shrinks to this: why did some places start growing at two per cent a year, and keep it up, while others did not?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8920
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|a||Economists have four main suspects.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2560
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1hs3occ'
    '|a||First, capital: machines, roads and factories, more tools for every worker.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5720
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:18yjtmt'
    '|a||Second, ideas: new technology, from steam engines to vaccines, that makes the same worker produce more.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7760
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:6m9uji'
    '|a||Third, institutions: the rules of the game. Who owns what, whether courts are fair, and whether those in power can be removed.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8400
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1g9iv94'
    '|a||Fourth, geography and culture: climate, disease, access to the sea, and how far people trust strangers.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8520
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:183s2vs'
    '|b||But capital and ideas are just how a country grows. The real fight is over why some countries get them and others don''t.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7200
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:r3b72x'
    '|a||And there the field splits. Daron Acemoglu, Simon Johnson and James Robinson argue that institutions rule: where colonisers set up rules that served only a few, countries are poorer today.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 13320
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1ag73ou'
    '|b||Jeffrey Sachs answers that geography also acts directly: where malaria is rife, incomes are lower, even with the same quality of government.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9160
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:hpw3uj'
    '|a||How sure are we? About the hockey stick and the divergence: consensus. About the deep causes: still contested.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8000
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1gd71wk'
    '|b||Three questions to try the idea on new cases. In 1945 Korea was split in two: at the split, the same geography, the same culture, the same history. By the best estimates, the South is now more than twenty times richer per person than the North. Which suspect does the Korean split point to? Tap it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 19000
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1u1kpqj'
    '|b||Three questions to try the idea on new cases. In 1945 Korea was split in two: at the split, the same geography, the same culture, the same history. By the best estimates, the South is now more than twenty times richer per person than the North. Which suspect does the Korean split point to?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 20440
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|b||Institutions. Geography and culture were shared; what differed after 1945 were the rules: markets and courts in the South, central planning under one party in the North.':
